# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its system config, which checks
# every text file out as CRLF. That fights patches/apply.py, which writes uosc's
# sources back as LF the way upstream ships them, and it makes any regex anchored
# on "$" miss the trailing \r. The repo is LF on both sides instead.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.jpg binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
